diagnosis_text:
The user reported recurrent network "freezes" attributed to core routers repeatedly dropping and re-forming OSPF adjacencies. A full SADE pass was completed across all four phases.

**Phase A (L2/Infrastructure):** infra_sweep and l2_snapshot returned zero anomalies. No ACL rules, no ARP poisoning, no duplicate MACs, no addressing inconsistencies. The only DOWN interfaces are those labeled "unused_*" in the topology — expected behavior.

**Phase B (Control plane):** ospf_snapshot confirmed all 9 FRR routers healthy (vtysh OK, both sockets present). Every adjacency is Full, every router carries 18 OSPF routes, and OSPF timers are uniformly standard (Hello 10 s / Dead 40 s / Wait 40 s). tc_snapshot found no bandwidth shaping or netem corruption on any interface.

**Phase C/D (Host and service):** pressure_sweep found no stress tools, no CPU spikes, no socket floods. dhcp_link_history found no flap events on any host. FRR/syslog logs on all routers are empty — no OSPF neighbor state-change events were ever recorded.

**Direct router inspection:** Bond interfaces on router16 and router17 (the only bonded uplinks) show Link Failure Count = 0 and carrier_changes = 2 (the startup-only baseline: down→up). OSPF neighbor detail shows exactly 6 state changes per neighbor — the textbook startup sequence (Down→Init→2-Way→ExStart→Exchange→Loading→Full) — with the most recent transition occurring ~4 minutes ago, consistent with initial lab startup convergence, not ongoing flapping.

**Root cause:** The reported symptom is unconfirmed. There is no injected fault, no flap mechanism, and no historical trace of the alleged instability. The network is fully stable.

**Fix:** No corrective action required. The network is operating normally; the reported intermittent behavior cannot be reproduced or attributed to any current or recently-active fault condition.
=== END ===